WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.540
- [Kim] Hey, this is
Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.410
And today we're learning
about McCulloch V. Maryland.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.270
A Supreme Court case
decided in 1819 that helped

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:11.880
to define the relationship

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.840
between the federal
government and the states.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.670
The question at issue
in this case was whether

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.810
the state of Maryland could
tax the Baltimore branch

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.020
of the Bank of the United States,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.470
and whether Congress even had the power

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.100
to create a Bank of the United
States in the first place.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.770
To learn more, I sought out
the help of two experts.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.110
Randy Barnett is the
Carmack Waterhouse Professor

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.010
of Legal Theory at the
Georgetown University Law Center

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.460
and Director of the Georgetown
Center for the Constitution.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.510
Neil Siegel is the David
W Ichel Professor of law

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.710
and Professor of Political
Science at Duke Law School.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:52.630
So, Professor Barnett,

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.530
could you kind of set the stage for us?

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.350
What was happening in this case?

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.880
What was the overall context?

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.430
- [Randy] Well, McCulloch V. Maryland

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.100
was a culmination

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.380
of a 30-year-old
constitutional controversy.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.170
In fact, it was the culmination

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.850
of one of the earliest controversies

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.030
that we had in the country.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.360
And that is over whether Congress had

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.010
the power to establish a National Bank.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.400
A bank that would be a
corporation formed by Congress,

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.320
which would have certain privileges

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.400
that Congress granted it.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.950
This was a proposal that had
been made by Alexander Hamilton

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.720
when he was Secretary of the Treasury

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.553
in the Washington Administration

00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:32.070
in the very first year of the
Washington Administration.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.760
And it went to Congress

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.180
and there was a very, very
robust debate in Congress

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.050
as to whether this measure

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.070
was within the powers
of Congress to enact.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.270
Eventually Congress voted that it was,

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.810
and then before he signed the bill,

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.580
President Washington asked
some of his cabinet members

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.220
to give him their opinion on
whether it was constitutional

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.390
and he heard from several
of his cabinet members.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.500
He heard from his Attorney
General Edmund Randolph,

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.070
he said it was unconstitutional.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.990
He heard from his Secretary
of State, Thomas Jefferson,

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.820
Jefferson said it was unconstitutional.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.590
Finally he heard from his
Secretary of Treasury,

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.770
who had proposed it, Alexander Hamilton,

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:10.730
who said it was constitutional,

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.270
and Washington signed the bill.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.550
And it became law and it established

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.110
the First National Bank
of the United States.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:21.980
- [Neil] This case arose in May of 1818

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.300
when Maryland sued McCulloch.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.830
And he was the cashier at the
Baltimore branch of the bank.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:30.250
Maryland sued him in state court

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:34.480
to recover a tax assessed
by Maryland on the bank.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.180
And this was a time of intense hostility

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.560
toward the National Bank
in a number of states.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.780
The state banks competed
with the National Bank

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.170
and there had been an
economic panic in 1818

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.740
when the US Bank called in
its loans and state banks

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.800
that had loans from the
Federal Bank were crippled.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.440
And in response, a number of states

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.820
passed nearly annihilative
taxes on the Federal Bank

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.410
and that's the environment
in which in 1819 McCulloch

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.009
against Maryland came
before the Supreme Court.

00:03:10.009 --> 00:03:10.842
- [Kim] And that's very interesting

00:03:10.842 --> 00:03:13.980
because this is something
that we talk a lot about

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.240
in the early 19th Century.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.390
The Bank of the United States
and what was good about it

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.760
and what was bad about it.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.880
There's certain people
who were certainly enemies

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.980
of the Bank like Thomas Jefferson,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.230
and then later, Andrew Jackson.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.240
Why did people object

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.543
to the Bank of the United States so much?

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.480
- [Neil] Yeah, there were
variety of objections.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.630
There was a real political
policy disagreement

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.540
about whether it was a good idea.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.540
Hamilton had a Nation-Building,
economy building objective,

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:52.790
as the first Secretary of the Treasury.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.100
He wanted to pay off
both the national debt

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.390
and the state debts from
the Revolutionary War

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.790
which remained unpaid.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.020
He was emphasizing
manufacturing in commerce.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.960
And the National Bank was
a key part of his plan.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.430
The bank would make it easier
for the national government

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.430
to raise taxes, to pay off
debts, to make payments,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.860
to obtain short term loans.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.390
The notes issued by
the bank could function

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.090
as a national currency.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.960
It could also provide a source of capital

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.620
for financing businesses.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.740
But the opponents had different ideas.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.030
- [Randy] One of the leading opponents

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.930
in Congress was James Madison,

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:34.350
who at the time was a representative

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.060
from Orange County, Virginia.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.200
What concerned him, and I think
what's concerned many people

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.840
was that there was no
expressed enumerative power

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.930
in the Constitution for
Congress to make a bank.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.550
There was an enumerative
power to create a post office,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.080
but there was no enumerated
power to create a bank.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.280
So the question is whether
the failure, or the silence,

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.490
of the constitution on
whether there was this power,

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.470
should be construed in favor
of having such a power,

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.800
or not having such a power.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.910
And Madison's concern was
that to imply such a power

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.100
especially when the way in
which it was being applied

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.510
was very remotely connected to
one of the enumerated powers

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.330
that were in the Constitution,
was very dangerous,

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.040
because by that form of reasoning,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.500
Congress could essentially
do whatever it wished

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.470
and that would violate the
basic pledge that this was going

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:28.890
to be a national government

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.720
of limited and enumerated powers.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.290
- [Kim] So, in Congress,
what was the power

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:39.260
that proponents of the bank
used to justify passing it?

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.523
- [Randy] The principal
power that they used

00:05:42.523 --> 00:05:44.868
is called the Necessary and Proper Clause.

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:45.967
The Necessary and Proper Clause says,

00:05:45.967 --> 00:05:48.217
"Congress shall have
Power to make all Laws

00:05:48.217 --> 00:05:50.827
"which shall be necessary
and proper for carrying

00:05:50.827 --> 00:05:52.920
"into Execution its foregoing Powers,"

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.487
those powers on the list,

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.777
"and all other Powers
vested by the Constitution

00:05:56.777 --> 00:05:58.057
"in the Government of the United States,

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:00.170
"or in any Department of Officer thereof."

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:02.680
This is called the
Necessary and Proper Clause.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.200
It allows for Congress
to make laws incidental

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.440
to the enumerated powers to effectuate,

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.960
or carry into execution
those powers and supporters

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.570
of the bill said that the bank carried

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.330
into execution a number of powers.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:17.983
It carried into execution
the taxing power,

00:06:17.983 --> 00:06:21.410
it carried into execution
the commerce power.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.950
The opponents of the bank
said, well, it may do that,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.800
but it only does that in
a very attenuated way.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.870
And therefore if it can do
this in order to effectuate

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.740
that power, then it pretty
much can do anything

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.850
to effectuate a power, and therefore

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:40.470
it can pretty much do anything
and that's a big problem.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:45.470
- [Kim] So, Maryland sues
McCulloch, and then what happens?

00:06:45.567 --> 00:06:47.530
- [Neil] Right, Maryland sues McCulloch

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.170
because Maryland didn't
pass an annihilative tax,

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:54.110
it was a tax of around 2% of the banknotes

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.000
issued by the National Bank.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.400
And Maryland won in the state trial court,

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.317
and Maryland won in
the state Supreme Court

00:07:02.317 --> 00:07:05.096
and this was really not
a surprise at the time.

00:07:05.096 --> 00:07:05.929
- [Kim] (laughs) Right.

00:07:05.929 --> 00:07:08.030
- [Neil] They were, let's just say

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:10.540
solicitous of the views of the state

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.690
and after the state Supreme Court decided,

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:16.610
the case went to the US
Supreme Court on appeal.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:19.240
So, even though Maryland sues McCulloch,

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:20.890
by the time it gets to the Supreme Court,

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:24.110
it's called McCulloch against
Maryland because McCulloch,

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:27.790
the cashier of the Baltimore
branch is now the petitioner.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:31.360
He is requesting the
US Supreme court review

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.000
of the decision of the
Maryland high court.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.780
And McCulloch is asking the
Supreme Court to intervene

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.500
and in essence side with
the Federal Government

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.690
over the state.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:46.000
- [Kim] Interesting, so what
are the constitutional issues

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.823
at stake once the McCulloch
case gets to the Supreme Court?

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:52.663
- [Randy] Well, the Supreme Court

00:07:52.663 --> 00:07:53.840
in a very lengthy opinion

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.350
has to consider a couple
of different matters.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.190
First, it has to consider
whether the states have the power

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:02.900
to tax a federal entity, like a bank,

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.080
and that's where you
have the famous statement

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.897
by John Marshal that says,

00:08:06.897 --> 00:08:10.050
"The power to tax can be
the power to destroy."

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.240
What he's talking about
is the power of states

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.500
to tax a federal entity,
like a bank, might be

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:17.620
the power of states to
destroy a federal entity

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.830
and he ruled against that claim.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.110
He basically argued that states

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:23.410
couldn't have that kind of power.

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.270
- [Neil] And a threshold question

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:26.890
before the Supreme Court decides

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:28.360
that question of state authority

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.070
to tax the National Government,

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:33.780
is whether the National Bank
can exist to begin with.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.120
Is there federal power to
create a national bank?

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:39.920
Part of what Maryland is arguing

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:43.210
is that there's no federal
power to create the bank.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:45.520
And so, in fact, this taxation

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.160
that it's engaging in is unproblematic.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.030
The first question is whether
the federal government

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:53.100
can create the bank,
and if the answer is no,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.440
then the case is over,
if the answer is yes,

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:56.710
then you get to the second question

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.220
of whether the states can tax

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.770
this part of the federal
government, the national bank.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.750
- [Kim] So at this time the Chief Justice

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:05.020
of the Supreme Court is John Marshal,

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:09.030
very well known as being
kind of the Chief Justice

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.900
that brought the Supreme
court to be a major player

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.920
in the US Governmental system.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.300
How did he interpret what was going on?

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.330
What did he and the other justices decide?

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.710
- [Neil] He borrowed extensively
from Hamilton's arguments.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:30.200
And so Marshal adopted
Hamilton's arguments

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.750
in defense of the
constitutionality of the bank

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:37.750
that Hamilton originally
articulated back in 1790, 1791.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:42.850
And so the court held, entered opinion

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:46.320
by the great Chief Justice
first that Congress does possess

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.650
the authority to create
the bank and secondly

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:52.660
that states have no
authority to tax the bank.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.620
- [Randy] And that is
what Marshall concluded

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:56.520
was within Congresses power

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:58.930
under the Necessary and
Proper Clause to enact.

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:00.770
And in fact, when it got to the court

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.090
in McCulloch V. Maryland,
it was the state of Maryland

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.560
who basically adopted
the Jeffersonian approach

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.210
and said that a measure
must be, in its words,

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.610
indispensably requisite
or what you might call

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:17.370
absolutely necessary in order
for it to be constitutional.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:21.350
And whereas the defenders
of the bank bill said

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.340
that it could be a lot less than that.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.530
So, I think that there's
basically three positions

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:27.363
that you can have.

00:10:27.363 --> 00:10:31.740
It has to be indispensably
requisite or logically necessary,

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:34.500
that's the Jefferson and Maryland view.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.030
It could be merely a matter
of convenience or expediency,

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:41.150
meaning basically Congress
can do whatever it wants.

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:44.450
That's the liberal view
that sometimes attributed

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:48.050
to John Marshal in McCulloch
V. Maryland, but he denied it.

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:50.150
And there's the in-between position

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:53.040
that I think both Madison
and Hamilton were favoring,

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.000
and that is the requirement of
some degree of means ends fit

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.640
so that a measure really is
aimed at a particular end,

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:04.030
and it's not really trying
to accomplish something

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:05.980
that Congress isn't given the power over.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:07.870
- [Neil] What I think is less well known

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:12.550
about this case is that this
part of the case was over

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:15.790
and the court had already
decided that this federal power

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:17.380
to create the bank before it even

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.130
got to the Necessary and Proper Clause.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:23.250
This case is a great
example of what's called

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:27.150
structural Constitutional Interpretation.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:30.650
Hamilton articulated two
structural principles,

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.730
first that the federal
government is supreme

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.000
within its sphere of action.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.000
And second if some kind of
end is within federal power

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:43.690
is listed in the Constitution,

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:47.260
then any convenient or
useful means to accomplishing

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:50.560
that end is also within the scope.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.830
And so, Marshal decides
that drawing inferences

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.470
from his understanding of
the theory and structure

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.470
of government created by the Constitution

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:03.950
and only after he does
that, does he then turn

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:07.540
to the Necessary and
Proper clause to confirm

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:09.840
what he has already deduced

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.463
through what he calls general reasoning.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:17.350
- [Kim] So, McCulloch V.
Maryland is frequently paired

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:21.300
with Marbury V. Madison as being two cases

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:25.880
that really decide the
extent of Federal Power

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:27.730
in this early era.

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:30.840
Do you think that these
two cases are related?

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:34.010
What do they tell us about the ideas

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:37.000
at this time period about federal power?

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:38.110
- [Randy] Well, they're very important.

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:39.670
I don't think they are quite

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:43.420
as extreme as they've come to
be read after the New Deal.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:47.430
When the New Deal Court
and progressive advocates

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:50.520
for a New Deal were going back

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.870
into the past and seeking justifications

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:54.170
for what they wanted to do,

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:57.080
they read McCulloch V.
Maryland very broadly.

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.940
They also read Marbury V. Madison.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.780
Actually they read Marbury V. Madison

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.160
in some respects very narrowly

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.120
because they didn't
want courts invalidating

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.790
their New Deal legislations.

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:09.350
Marbury V. Madison was not a huge deal

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:10.620
at the time it was decided.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:14.270
The idea that judges had a
duty to follow the higher law

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.710
when it was in conflict
with the mere statute,

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.870
was widely accepted at
the time of the founding.

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:22.410
And so Marbury was not
announcing a new policy.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:24.270
McCulloch V. Maryland on the other hand

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:27.120
was extremely controversial
when it was decided,

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:30.570
and in fact quite oddly

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:34.280
James Madison, who had signed the bill

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:38.180
authorizing the second
bank into law, greatly,

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:41.270
strenuously objected to
John Marshall's opinion

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:43.800
in McCulloch V. Maryland
upholding the bill

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.860
that Madison had signed into law.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:49.750
So, Madison still maintained that Marshall

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:53.930
had a latitudinarian or
overly broad interpretation

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.360
of federal power, even
in upholding the bill

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:57.670
that Madison by this time

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.240
had come to believe was constitutional.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:01.660
- [Kim] So, what ultimately happened

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:04.120
with the Bank of the United States?

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:05.550
- [Neil] What ultimately happened

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:08.560
is both the first bank, the
bill creating the first bank

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.700
and the second bank had what's
called a sunset provision,

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.777
which means after a
certain amount of time,

00:14:13.777 --> 00:14:16.490
and it was 20 years, it expires.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:19.620
And so to reauthorize it, it
puts the burden of inertia

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.210
on those who want the thing to continue.

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:24.560
And so Congress had to pass another bill

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:26.390
reauthorizing the bank.

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:30.970
And president Andrew Jackson
opposed reauthorization.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:33.290
- [Randy] President Andrew Jackson vetoed

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:35.310
the reauthorization of the bank.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:37.040
And it was very interesting

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.480
because he vetoed it on
constitutional grounds.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.690
He said it was unconstitutional and yet,

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.840
what happened to Mark
McCulloch V. Maryland?

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:47.370
If McCulloch V. Maryland said
the bank was constitutional,

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.660
how could President Jackson say

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.100
that it was unconstitutional?

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.300
Well, it was interesting
because what McCulloch said

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:57.590
was that the bank was
constitutional as an exercise

00:14:57.590 --> 00:14:59.850
of Congress' power to make laws

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.600
that are Necessary and Proper,

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.100
that Congress believed
was Necessary and Proper.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:06.440
And because Congress
believed it was necessary

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.650
and because the measure
was plainly adapted

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:12.070
to a legitimate end in the Constitution,

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:13.700
then it was constitutional.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:17.140
What Jackson said was,
hey, look, the court said

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:18.600
that it's up to Congress to decide

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:21.840
whether something is necessary
and therefore constitutional,

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:25.080
and I as President, exercise a veto power

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:26.870
as part of the legislative process,

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:29.300
therefore it is up to me to decide

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:30.860
whether the measure is necessary

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.040
and therefore is constitutional,
so I as President,

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:38.720
have decided that a bank is
not necessary and therefore,

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:41.820
because the bank is not
necessary it is unconstitutional,

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.800
and McCulloch V. Maryland
allows me, as a participant

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:48.490
in the legislative
process, to make that call.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:50.240
- [Neil] Congress did not override

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:52.860
Jackson's veto and the bank expired,

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.960
and the story ended in 1836.

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:59.070
And I think this speaks
to one of many morals

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:01.430
of the story of the Bank
of the United States.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:04.210
The Supreme Court doesn't
have the last word

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:08.070
on constitutional questions
when it upholds exercises

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:11.640
of federal power, it's then
left to the political process

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.530
to decide whether or
not it wants to continue

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:19.290
whatever controversial action
or legislation was at issue.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:22.820
- [Kim] So, is there any
aspect of McCulloch V. Maryland

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:24.330
that still affects us today?

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:27.170
- [Randy] One reading of McCulloch
is that it gives Congress

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:30.780
a power so broad that it allows Congress

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:35.780
to exercise any power
that it deems convenient,

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.730
to the exercise of one of
it's enumerative powers.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.750
That is how McCulloch has
come to be interpreted.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:45.350
I think that is an over
reading of McCulloch.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:49.570
And it also overlooks
one of the key passages

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:51.970
of McCulloch V. Maryland that nowadays

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:55.003
is given no legal effect
by the Supreme Court.

00:16:55.003 --> 00:16:57.887
This is what John Marshal
said, "Should Congress,

00:16:57.887 --> 00:17:00.977
"under the pretext of
executing its powers,

00:17:00.977 --> 00:17:04.197
"pass laws for the
accomplishment of objects

00:17:04.197 --> 00:17:06.827
"not entrusted to the
government, it would become

00:17:06.827 --> 00:17:08.967
"the painful duty of this tribunal,

00:17:08.967 --> 00:17:11.777
"should a case requiring such
a decision come before it,

00:17:11.777 --> 00:17:16.350
"to say that such an act was
not the law of the land."

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:18.910
So what Marshall is saying there is

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:23.910
that just because Congress
says a law is necessary

00:17:24.370 --> 00:17:27.260
to one of it's enumerative
powers, doesn't make it so,

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:29.430
and if there is a lack of fit

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.660
between means and ends suggesting

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:34.610
that in fact Congress is trying
to pursue one of the powers

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:37.740
that was not given to it
under the Constitution,

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.730
it really would be the
painful duty of the tribunal

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:42.350
to say it was not the law of the land,

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:46.080
and that connects this case
back up with Marbury V. Madison

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.590
in which it is the painful duty
of the Supreme court to say

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:51.460
that a statute is not the law of the land

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.010
if it is unconstitutional.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:57.190
That aspect of McCulloch V.
Maryland is no longer followed

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:59.370
in part because during the New Deal,

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:01.790
the Supreme Court said
that it would not inquire

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:05.670
into the motives of
Congress in enacting laws.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:09.070
And in fact, what McCulloch
is saying here is to inquire

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:10.620
into the motives is to say, hey, look,

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:12.950
it's really, it purports
to be doing one thing,

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:15.830
but it's really doing something else

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:17.580
and that is pre textual.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.170
- [Neil] Yeah, there
has been irreconcilable

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.760
disagreement on basic
constitutional questions

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:23.610
from the very beginning

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.650
of the constitution.
(Kim Laughs)

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:28.470
Madison and Hamilton, who
come together and write

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.600
the federalist papers, they disagree

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:32.090
about this fundamental question

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.040
of strict versus loose construction

00:18:34.040 --> 00:18:35.980
of Congress's numerated powers.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:39.080
They also disagree about
Congress's spending powers.

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:41.900
They disagree about
inherent executive powers.

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:46.900
So sometimes originalist
constitutional arguments presuppose

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.860
a greater degree of consensus

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.550
about what the constitution means

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:54.830
at the start of the
country that I don't see

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.500
when I study the history.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:59.710
We have always disagreed,
we've always managed

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:02.990
to find some kind of
community in disagreement.

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:04.600
It's the conflicts and disagreements

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:09.160
that have binded us together
as much, if not more,

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:13.600
than agreements we've had about
what the Constitution means.

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:16.040
- [Kim] So, we've learned
that McCulloch V. Maryland

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:19.000
was about far more than
just a tax on a bank.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.610
It bolstered the power
of the federal government

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:25.220
by broadly defining the
Necessary and Proper Clause,

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:29.940
and by confirming that federal
law is supreme to state law.

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.370
To learn more about McCulloch V. Maryland,

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:34.570
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:37.730
interactive constitution
and Khan Academy's resources

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:39.723
on US Government and politics.

